                            2. ELIGIBLE CHURCH

2.1 An Eligible Church is a nonprofit religious congregation, or a council,
    district, association or denomination body acting for congregations, that is
    lawfully established under the law where it is located, is organized and
    operated principally for congregational worship and related religious
    ministry, has identifiable governance, and distributes no net earnings for
    private benefit. No particular denomination or government recognition letter
    is required, and none is conclusive.

2.2 An Eligible Church does not include a body formed or controlled chiefly to
    obtain rights under this license, a commercial affiliate acting for its own
    account, or a company, school, broadcaster or nonprofit whose programming is
    not that congregation's own worship, teaching and ministry.

2.3 Verification. Eligibility is established by a written request from an
    authorized person of the congregation that names the congregation, its
    location, the requesting officer, the installations or channels to be
    covered, and a statement that the congregation meets section 2.1. Send it
    to the contact in section 12. The Licensor confirms eligibility in writing
    and may ask for reasonable supporting documentation appropriate to the
    jurisdiction; a maintainer's silence is not approval. Verification data is
    used only to decide and record eligibility: the Licensor collects the
    minimum needed, does not sell it, does not require member lists or personal
    data of a congregation's members, and deletes it on written request except
    for a record needed to evidence a granted permission.

2.4 Disputes. A written decision under section 2.3 may be challenged once, in
    writing, within thirty days, with the facts relied on. The Licensor answers
    in writing within thirty days. Section 12.2 applies. No congregation is
    required to litigate to keep a compliant grant.

                        3. ROYALTY-FREE CHURCH GRANT

3.1 Subject to sections 6, 7 and 9, an Eligible Church receives a worldwide,
    nonexclusive, royalty-free copyright license to inspect, copy, install, run,
    modify and internally deploy Covered Software for its own congregational
    worship, religious teaching, communications, accessibility and related
    nonprofit ministry, including free public access to that congregation's own
    programming through television and web applications. A body described in
    section 2.1 may deploy Covered Software for the congregations it serves, and
    each such congregation then uses it under that body's grant.

3.2 There is no software license fee, no mandatory subscription, no channel cap,
    no viewer cap and no requirement to buy managed hosting. A church may run
    Covered Software on its own equipment or on ordinary third-party
    infrastructure. Soliciting offerings, tithes and voluntary donations for the
    congregation does not by itself make a permitted use commercial.

3.3 Not included in section 3.1: selling or sublicensing access to a software
    service, reselling the application, charging for access to programming or an
    archive as a business, operating an unrelated business, or advertising
    operations run for third parties.

3.4 Distribution of the church's own application binaries. An Eligible Church
    may distribute binaries built from Covered Software, under the
    congregation's own branding, to its congregation and to the public,
    including through app stores, for the purposes in section 3.1, subject to
    sections 7 and 8. Store operators receive only the incidental permission to
    host, review, test and distribute those permitted binaries; they receive no
    independent right to operate, modify, resell or sublicense the platform.

3.5 End users of a permitted build. A person who receives an application built
    under section 3.1 or 3.4 may install and run that build on their own
    devices, at no charge and without accepting this license in writing, to
    watch that church's programming. The permission covers running the build as
    distributed. It does not permit modifying or republishing the software,
    reselling it, hosting the platform, or operating a channel of one's own, and
    it conveys no right to the church's content (section 6.4).

                  4. CHURCH AGENTS AND INFRASTRUCTURE

4.1 Employees, volunteers and independent contractors may install, host, maintain
    or customize an Eligible Church's own instance solely as that church's agent,
    and may be paid reasonable compensation for that work, including running that
    one congregation's instance on infrastructure the agent controls and charging
    for that service. The church is responsible for its agents observing this
    license.

4.2 An agent receives no independent right to resell Covered Software, keep an
    independently exploitable platform, run a multitenant or white-label service
    for multiple customers from one church's eligibility, or reuse one church's
    eligibility for another customer. The boundary is concrete: serving one
    congregation, or the congregations of one body under section 2.1, is agent
    work; serving several unrelated customers at a time, or marketing hosting as
    the agent's own product, is a channel-hosting business under section 6.1 and
    needs rights from the Licensor.

4.3 Infrastructure and store vendors may charge their normal compute, storage,
    bandwidth, support and distribution fees without receiving commercial
    rights in Covered Software. These narrow exceptions do not authorize a
    competing managed CTV or channel-hosting business: software that serves
    several customers from a shared platform, or that is marketed as the
    operator's own hosting product, needs separate rights from the Licensor -
    even a service aimed only at churches, and even one charged at cost.

              5. EVALUATION, FORKS AND CONTRIBUTIONS

5.1 Any person may inspect this source, make private local copies and build and
    run it locally to evaluate it or to prepare contributions, provided the
    person does not operate a production channel, serve a customer, generate
    revenue, or obtain a commercial operational benefit. Internal production use
    by a company is not evaluation. This section grants no rights to run a
    public nonchurch channel, a paid proof of concept, a commercial integration,
    or any service.

5.2 A private fork or branch made under section 5.1 stays subject to this
    license. Publishing a fork does not enlarge these permissions.

5.3 Contributions are accepted only from a contributor who has accepted the
    Contributor Agreement published in this repository, by the recorded method
    stated in that agreement and in CONTRIBUTING.md. A submission alone is not a
    transfer of copyright and is not a warranty that the contributor had the
    right to submit.

                6. COMMERCIAL RESTRICTION AND RESERVED RIGHTS

6.1 Except for the narrow church-agent, end-user, infrastructure, store and
    evaluation exceptions stated above (sections 3.5, 4.1 to 4.3 and 5.1), this
    license grants no right to use Covered Software commercially. Commercial use includes commercial self-hosting, commercial
    internal operations, resale, sublicensing, white-label or rebranded products,
    managed hosting, channel factories, and any service furnished to a third
    party for money or other economic benefit.

6.2 Nonprofit status, absence of profit, charitable customers, sponsorship
    income or free-trial pricing does not by itself authorize a use excluded by
    section 6.1. A nonprofit or government body that is not an Eligible Church
    does not acquire the church grant automatically.

6.3 The Licensor may operate paid services and may separately grant rights in
    material the Licensor owns or is authorized to license, including commercial
    self-hosting and managed-service rights. Buying a service grants a customer
    no commercial rights in Covered Software unless a written agreement says so.
    This license promises no particular commercial license or price.

6.4 Content, branding and trademarks. No right to use project names, trademarks,
    logos, endorsements, app-store identities or signing credentials is granted,
    except accurate factual attribution and uses separately authorized in
    writing. A church keeps its own marks and content, and grants the Licensor
    the right to state factually that the church uses the software. Churches
    supply their own branding or use assets expressly identified as
    redistributable.

                           7. NOTICES AND SHARE-BACK

7.1 Retain this license, the copyright line, and all third-party license, notice
    and attribution texts. Identify modifications clearly, and do not present a
    modified build as an official or Licensor-supported release.

7.2 Share-back. If You distribute a modified version of Covered Software, or
    deploy a modification of Covered Software into operational use (including
    use on a church network or a church-hosted channel), then within thirty days
    You must make the corresponding modified source of that Covered Software,
    together with the build and install instructions You use, available under
    this same license at a public link You state, and notify the upstream
    project of that link through its published contribution channel. Keep it
    available while the distribution or deployment continues and for three years
    afterwards. No maintainer is obliged to merge or support Your change, and
    publishing source does not enlarge the permitted uses of it.

7.3 Share-back covers modifications of Covered Software and the scripts needed
    to build and install them. It never requires You to publish sermons or other
    content, member or viewer data, personal information, credentials, private
    keys, commercial terms, ordinary channel configuration, or independently
    developed software that only communicates with Covered Software through a
    documented interface. Publish safe configuration templates instead. An
    undeployed private experiment has no publication duty until it is deployed
    operationally or distributed.

7.4 Telemetry and data. Any telemetry in Covered Software is opt-in and
    configurable. Churches remain responsible for their own privacy notices,
    consent and data handling for their viewers and members.

                              8. PATENTS

8.1 To the extent the Licensor can grant them, the copyright grants above include
    a nonexclusive, royalty-free, worldwide patent license limited to claims
    necessarily infringed by the unmodified Covered Software as used in a
    permitted way. No other patent rights are implied.

8.2 If You begin a patent or patent-like proceeding asserting that Covered
    Software infringes a patent You hold or may license, the patent grant in
    section 8.1 to You ends as of the filing date, to the extent the law allows.
    Defensive filings by an Eligible Church against a claim brought against it
    do not trigger this section.

                     9. COMPLIANCE AND TERMINATION

9.1 The conditions in sections 2 to 7 are conditions of the grants. A material
    breach suspends the affected permissions. A first inadvertent, curable
    breach may be cured within thirty days after written notice by stopping the
    prohibited conduct and correcting the breach; the grant is then reinstated
    automatically for the affected version.

9.2 An uncured or repeated material breach, or deliberate commercial operation
    outside this license, terminates the affected grants. Termination does not
    reach a compliant downstream congregation acting under its own eligible
    grant, and does not excuse the share-back duty for a modification already
    distributed or deployed while the grant was in force.

9.3 Versions. Each released version of Covered Software is licensed under the
    license version shipped with it. A later license version does not silently
    change, narrow or revoke the grant for a version already received, and no
    license version can enlarge the Licensor's rights in third-party material or
    erase an earlier written grant. The Licensor may publish new versions.

